[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Compute $\cos 180^\circ$.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To compute \(\cos 180^\circ\), we need to understand the trigonometric identity for cosine at specific angles. The cosine of an angle is defined as the ratio of the adjacent side to the hypotenuse in a right-angled triangle corresponding to that angle. For a \(60^\circ\) angle (which is the supplementary angle to \(180^\circ\)), we have \(\cos 60^\circ = \frac{1}{2}\).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m For a \(180^\circ\) angle (which is the same as \(360^\circ - 180^\circ = 180^\circ\) or a circle), the cosine of the angle is \(\cos 180^\circ = -1\).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Thus, \(\cos 180^\circ = -1\).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Putting the final answer in the required \(\boxed{}\), we get:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \(\boxed{-1}\)<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m [Ground Truth]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m -1
