[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Tyler has entered a buffet line in which he chooses one kind of meat, two different vegetables and one dessert.  If the order of food items is not important, how many different meals might he choose?
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m $\bullet$  Meat: beef, chicken, pork
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m $\bullet$  Vegetables: baked beans, corn, potatoes, tomatoes
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m $\bullet$  Dessert: brownies, chocolate cake, chocolate pudding, ice cream
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Tyler has 3 meat options, 4 vegetable options, and 4 dessert options available.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Since the order of food items is not important, we need to use the formula for permutations of multiple items with repetition. This formula is given by:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \text{Permutations} = \frac{n!}{m_1! \cdot m_2! \cdot m_3!}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m where \( n \) is the total number of items, and \( m_1, m_2, m_3 \) are the number of each type of item. In this problem:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - \( n = 3 \) (meat options)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - \( m_1 = 1 \) (boiled beans)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - \( m_2 = 2 \) (corn, potatoes, tomatoes)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - \( m_3 = 1 \) (chocolate pudding or ice cream)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Substituting these values into the formula, we get:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \text{Permutations} = \frac{3!}{1! \cdot 2! \cdot 1!}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m First, calculate \( 3! \):
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 3! = 3 \times 2 \times 1 = 6
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Next, calculate \( 2! \):
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 2! = 2 \times 1 = 2
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Now substitute these values back into the formula:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \text{Permutations} = \frac{6}{1 \cdot 2 \cdot 1} = \frac{6}{2} = 3
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Therefore, the number of different meals Tyler might choose is:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \boxed{3}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
