[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m From the previous question, if a B-cell tumor progresses and splits into two smaller B-cells with mutated surface IgA molecules, the resulting cell population is called coagglutinated B-cells. Assuming each coagglutinated cell divides into two BA cells every 24 hours, and each BA cell also divides into two BA cells every 30 minutes, calculate the aggregate number of BA cells that circulate in the body starting with 10 coagglutinated cells at t=48.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To solve this problem, we need to understand the process of coagglutination and then determine how the number of coagglutinated cells changes over time. Each coagglutinated cell splits into two BA cells every 24 hours, and each BA cell also splits into two BA cells every 30 minutes. The final description is that each BA cell splits into two BA cells every 60 minutes.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m First, let's determine how many BA cells are formed by a single coagglutinated B-cell at \( t = 48 \) hours:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Complete split: \( 10 - 5 = 5 \) coagglutinated cells. So, \( 2 \times 5 = 10 \) BA cells are formed initially from each coagglutinated B-cell. Next, each of these 10 BA cells splits into two BA cells every 24 hours. This means that \( 10 \times 2 = 20 \) BA cells are formed per 24 hours. Therefore, over the course of 48 hours, the number of BA cells around the coagglutinated B-cells will be \( 20 \times 48 = 960 \).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Next, we need to determine how many BA cells are formed by these 960 BA cells at \( t = 0 \):
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Complete split: \( 960 - 48 = 912 \) coagglutinated cells. So, \( 2 \times 912 = 1824 \) BA cells are formed initially from each coagglutinated B-cells. Next, each of these 1824 BA cells splits into two BA cells every 24 hours. This means that \( 1824 \times 2 = 3648 \) BA cells are formed per 24 hours. Therefore, over the course of 0 hours, the number of BA cells around the coagglutinated B-cells will be \( 3648 \).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Thus, the aggregate number of BA cells that circulate in the body starting with 10 coagglutinated cells at \( t = 48 \) is \( \boxed{3648} \).
