[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m A block of cheese in the shape of a rectangular solid measures $10$ cm by $13$ cm by $14$ cm. Ten slices are cut from the cheese. Each slice has a width of $1$ cm and is cut parallel to one face of the cheese. The individual slices are not necessarily parallel to each other. What is the maximum possible volume in cubic cm of the remaining block of cheese after ten slices have been cut off?
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To determine the maximum possible volume of the remaining block of cheese after ten slices have been cut off from it, we need to follow these steps:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 1. **Calculate the initial volume of the cheese:**
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    The cheese forms a rectangular solid with dimensions \(10 \text{ cm} \times 13 \text{ cm} \times 14 \text{ cm}\).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \text{Volume} = 10 \times 13 \times 14 = 2040 \text{ cm}^3
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 2. **Determine the dimensions of the smaller slices:**
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    Each slice has a width of 1 cm.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    - When a slice is cut at a 1 cm distance from one face, the length of the smaller slice on the \(x\)-axis will be \(10 - 1 = 9 \text{ cm}\) (since it has a width of 1 cm), and the width will remain at 1 cm.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    - When a slice is cut at a 1 cm distance from one of the \(y\)-faces or \(z\)-faces, the length of the smaller slice on the \(y\)-axis will be \(13 - 1 = 12 \text{ cm}\) (since it has a width of 1 cm) or the width will be 1 cm (on the \(z\)-face).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 3. **Evaluate the effects of cutting along the \(y\)-axis and \(z\)-axis:**
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    - Cutting along the \(y\)-axis will remove a unit length in the \(y\)-direction from the remaining \(y\)-space, resulting in a decrease in volume by 9 cm in the \(x\)-direction.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    - Cutting along the \(z\)-axis will remove a unit length in the \(z\)-direction from the remaining \(z\)-space, resulting in a decrease in volume by 12 cm in the \(x\)-direction.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 4. **Calculate the remaining volume:**
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    After cutting the slices, the \(y\)-slice removes 9 cm from the \(y\)-axis, and the \(z\)-slice removes 12 cm from the \(z\)-axis. Therefore, the remaining \(y\)-space is \(13 - 12 = 1 \text{ cm}\) shorter, and the remaining \(z\)-space is \(14 - 9 = 5 \text{ cm}\) shorter.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \text{Remaining volume} = 14 \times 9 - 1 \times 12 = 126 \text{ cm}^3
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Thus, the maximum possible volume of the remaining block of cheese after ten slices have been cut off is \(\boxed{126}\).<|endoftext|>
