[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Let's denote by \(f(n)\) the number of different sums that can be made to get exactly \(n\) cents. The key observation here is that for any sum \(n\), \(n\) can be written as \(a_k + a_{k-1} + \cdots + a_1 + a_0\), where \(k\) is the number of dimes used and \(a_i\) are the units digits of the dimes \(i\). Since \(k\) is an integer, \(n\) can be written in terms of the units digits of \(k\) (if possible), or it can be written as the sum of \(k\) units. For \(n\) to be achievable, \(k\) must be chosen appropriately, and the calculation for \(f(n)\) will be the number of ways to represent \(n\) as a sum of some integers.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m The sum can be expressed as:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[n = a_k \cdot 10^k + a_{k-1} \cdot 10^{k-1} + \cdots + a_1 \cdot 10 + a_0 \cdot 1.\]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Since \(a_k \leq 9\) (all dimes are \(1\) carat), the sum is maximized when all others are minimized. Therefore, we can think that \(n\) is limited by the number of consecutive dimes used to get the smallest possible number of cents. The maximum value of \(n\) is when all dimes are used, in which case \(n = \sum_{i=1}^k i \cdot 10^k\). This is an arithmetic series:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[n = 1 \cdot 10^k + 2 \cdot 10^{k-1} + \cdots + k \cdot 10^0.\]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m We can solve for this sum:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[n = \frac{10^k \cdot (k + 1) - k \sum_{i=1}^k 10^i}{9}.\]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m This is the formula for the number of different sums \(f(n)\) that can be obtained in terms of \(n\).
