[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To determine the greatest number of inches possible in the length of each piece when the three ropes are cut into equal length pieces, we need to find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of the lengths of the ropes. The lengths of the ropes are 39 inches, 52 inches, and 65 inches.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m First, we find the prime factorization of each number:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - The prime factorization of 39 is \(3 \times 13\).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - The prime factorization of 52 is \(2^2 \times 13\).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - The prime factorization of 65 is \(5 \times 13\).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m The GCD is the product of the lowest power of each prime factor that appears in all three factorizations. Here, the only common prime factor is 13, and it appears in both 39 and 52, but not in the number 65. Therefore, the GCD is 13.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Now, we divide each length of the rope by the GCD to find out how many pieces of equal length can be cut, and how many inches will be left over:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - \(39 \div 13 = 3\) pieces with 0 inches left over.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - \(52 \div 13 = 4\) pieces with 0 inches left over.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - \(65 \div 13 = 5\) pieces with 0 inches left over.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m In each of the 3 cuts, the ropes are split into pieces of 13 inches long. Therefore, the greatest number of inches possible in the length of each piece is 13, and it will be with no left over.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Thus, the greatest number of inches possible in the length of each piece is \(\boxed{14}\).<|endoftext|>
