[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Determine the KL Divergence between the empirical distributions $\{0.731,0.107,0.33\}$ and $\{0.762,0.185,0.274\}$.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To determine the Kullback-Leibler divergence (KL Divergence) between the empirical distributions \(\{0.731, 0.107, 0.33\}\) and \(\{0.762, 0.185, 0.274\}\), we need to follow these steps:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 1. **Calculate the KL Divergence Function**:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    The KL Divergence between two probability distributions \(P\) and \(Q\) is defined as:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    D_{KL}(P \| Q) = \frac{1}{2} \sum_{i} P(i) \ln \left(\frac{P(i)}{Q(i)}\right)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    Here, \(P = \{0.731, 0.107, 0.33\}\) and \(Q = \{0.762, 0.185, 0.274\}\).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 2. **Compute each term in the sum**:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    For each element \(i\):
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    - \(P(0) = 0.731\), \(Q(0) = 0.762\)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    - \(P(1) = 0.107\), \(Q(1) = 0.185\)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    - \(P(2) = 0.33\), \(Q(2) = 0.274\)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 3. **Calculate each term**:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    P(0) \ln \left(\frac{P(0)}{Q(0)}\right) = 0.731 \ln \left(\frac{0.731}{0.762}\right)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    P(1) \ln \left(\frac{P(1)}{Q(1)}\right) = 0.107 \ln \left(\frac{0.107}{0.185}\right)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    P(2) \ln \left(\frac{P(2)}{Q(2)}\right) = 0.33 \ln \left(\frac{0.33}{0.274}\right)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 4. **Simplify each term**:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    P(0) \ln \left(\frac{P(0)}{Q(0)}\right) = 0.731 \ln \left(0.9466\right)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    P(1) \ln \left(\frac{P(1)}{Q(1)}\right) = 0.107 \ln \left(0.5665\right)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    P(2) \ln \left(\frac{P(2)}{Q(2)}\right) = 0.33 \ln \left(1.4736\right)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 5. **Calculate the logarithms**:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    0.9466 = 0.9466
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    0.5665 = 0.5665
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    1.4736 = 1.4736
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 6. **Compute each term**:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    0.731 \ln \left(\frac{0.731}{0.762}\right) \approx 0.731 \times (-0.1303) = -0.0950
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    0.107 \ln \left(\frac{0.107}{0.185}\right) \approx 0.107 \times (-0.2400) = -0.0268
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    0.33 \ln \left(\frac{0.33}{0.274}\right) \approx 0.33 \times (-0.8146) = -0.2612
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 7. **Sum the terms**:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    -0.0950 + (-0.0268) + (-0.2612) \approx -0.383
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 8. **Take the absolute value and divide by 2**:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    D_{KL}(P \| Q) = \left| -0.383 \right| / 2 \approx 0.1915
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Therefore, the KL Divergence between the empirical distributions \(\{0.731, 0.107, 0.33\}\) and \(\{0.762, 0.185, 0.274\}\) is approximately \(\boxed{0.1915}\).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m ****
