[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To determine the image of the vector \(\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix}\) under a dilation centered at the origin with scale factor \(k\), we need to follow these steps:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 1. Identify the scaling factor \(k\). The dilation takes \(\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ 7 \end{pmatrix}\) to \(\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\ -21 \end{pmatrix}\). Therefore, \(k = \frac{-12}{4} = -3\).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 2. Apply the dilation to the vector \(\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix}\):
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix} \cdot k = \begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix} \cdot (-3) = -2 \cdot (-3) = 6
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    This means that \(\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix}\) is dilated to \(\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}\).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 3. Since the dilation is centered at the origin, the dilation of \(\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}\) to \(\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix}\) is the same as dilating \(\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}\) to \(\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix}\).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Therefore, the vector that the dilation takes \(\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix}\) to is \(\boxed{\begin{pmatrix}6 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}}\).<|endoftext|>
