[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To determine how long it will take my friend to read the same novella, we need to follow these steps:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 1. **Calculate the speed of my friend:**
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    - I take 2 hours to read a novella.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    - Since it takes me 2 hours to read the novella, my friend will read it in 2 × 60 = 120 minutes.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 2. **Determine the reading speed of my friend:**
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    - My friend reads three times as fast as I do.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    - Therefore, my friend reads at a rate of \(\frac{1}{120}\) novellas per minute.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 3. **Convert the reading time from hours to minutes:**
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    - I read the novella in 2 hours.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    - Since 1 hour = 60 minutes, 2 hours = 2 × 60 = 120 minutes.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 4. **Calculate the time my friend will take to read the novella:**
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    - My friend reads 120 minutes at a rate of \(\frac{1}{120}\) novellas per minute.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    - Therefore, my friend will take \(\frac{120}{\frac{1}{120}} = 120 \times 120 = 14400\) minutes.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Thus, it will take my friend \(\boxed{14400}\) minutes to read the same novella.<|endoftext|>
