[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Of the numbers 1, 2, 3, ..., 15, which number has the greatest number of divisors (the dots mean that we are including all the integers between 1 and 15)?
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To determine which number has the greatest number of divisors, we need to consider the prime factorization of each number. The number of divisors of a number \( n \) with prime factorization \( n = p_1^{e_1} p_2^{e_2} \cdots p_k^{e_k} \) is given by \( (e_1 + 1)(e_2 + 1) \cdots (e_k + 1) \).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Let's list the numbers from 1 to 15 and their prime factorizations:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \begin{aligned}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m &1 = 1 \quad & \text{(divisors: 1)} \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m &2 = 2 \quad & \text{(divisors: 1, 2)} \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m &3 = 3 \quad & \text{(divisors: 1, 3)} \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m &4 = 2^2 \quad & \text{(divisors: 1, 2, 4)} \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m &5 = 5 \quad & \text{(divisors: 1, 5)} \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m &6 = 2 \cdot 3 \quad & \text{(divisors: 1, 2, 3, 6)} \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m &7 = 7 \quad & \text{(divisors: 1, 7)} \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m &8 = 2^3 \quad & \text{(divisors: 1, 2, 4, 8)} \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m &9 = 3^2 \quad & \text{(divisors: 1, 3, 9)} \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m &10 = 2 \cdot 5 \quad & \text{(divisors: 1, 2, 5, 10)} \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m &11 = 11 \quad & \text{(divisors: 1, 11)} \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m &12 = 2^2 \cdot 3 \quad & \text{(divisors: 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 12)} \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m &13 = 13 \quad & \text{(divisors: 1, 13)} \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m &14 = 2 \cdot 7 \quad & \text{(divisors: 1, 2, 7, 14)} \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m &15 = 3 \cdot 5 \quad & \text{(divisors: 1, 3, 5, 15)} \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \end{aligned}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Now, let's calculate the number of divisors for each number:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \begin{aligned}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m &1: & \text{(1)} & \text{(1 divisor)} \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m &2: & \text{(2)} & \text{(2 divisors)} \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m &3: & \text{(3)} & \text{(3 divisors)} \\
