[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m This indicates there was a misunderstanding; the sum of the right angles plus the remaining two angles should sum to \( 180^\circ \). Let's correct this by ensuring the sum of angles in a pentagon includes all angles, not just the right angles.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m The correct approach is to recognize that the sum of the angles taken directly (excluding the right angles) should still add up to \( 540^\circ \). The right angle is indeed \( 90^\circ \), and the remaining angles add up to \( 540^\circ - 90^\circ = 450^\circ \). Since the pentagon has three right angles and one angle is \( 45^\circ \), the remaining two angles must be \( 45^\circ \).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Thus, the measure of one of these other angles is:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
