[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m D: $\frac{12}{13}$
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To find the cosine of the angle \(\alpha\) whose terminal side passes through the point \(P(5, -12)\), we need to use the definition of the cosine function in terms of the coordinates of the point on the unit circle. The cosine of an angle \(\alpha\) in the coordinate plane is given by the x-coordinate of the point where the terminal side of the angle intersects the unit circle.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m The x-coordinate of the point \(P(5, -12)\) is 5, and the radius (or distance from the origin to the point) is \(\sqrt{5^2 + (-12)^2} = \sqrt{25 + 144} = \sqrt{169} = 13\).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Therefore, the cosine of the angle \(\alpha\) is the x-coordinate of the point, which is 5.
