[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m How much can you decrease Can $5/\log 5$ by by considering the following binomial expansion?
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[\begin{array}{ccc}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m (x + 6)^x &=& a + ax^2 + O(x^3)\\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m (x + f)^x &=& b + bx^2 + O(x^3)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \end{array}\]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m I know that $f = 6$
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m How can I use the binomial expansion to find $b$ and $a$? Also, how can I determine the correct upper limit for the term $bx^2$? The general method is still to consider the power of $x=5$. From Wolfram, it gives the following Formulas for the limit of $n^\frac1n$ as $n$ approaches infinity:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m $$\lim_{n\to +\infty}\left(n^{1/n}\right)^{0.5} = 1 \tag{1}\label{1}\tag{1}$$
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m whereas for power of $x=x^{0.5}$ :
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m $$\lim_{n\to +\infty}\left(n^{x^{1/x}}\right)^{1/x} = 1 \tag{2}\label{2}$$
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m whereas for our power of $x=\frac15\log 5$ :
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m $$\lim_{n\to +\infty}\left(\left(\frac{5}{6}\right)^x\right)^{\frac{1}{\log 5}} = 1 \tag{3}\label{3}$$
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m which gives us a specific formula:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m $$\lim_{n\to +\infty}\left(\left(\frac{5}{6}\right)^{\frac{1}{x}}\right)^{x+n-1} = 1 \tag{4}\label{4}$$
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m or, by squaring both sides :
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m $$\left[\left(\frac{5}{6}\right)^x\right]^{\frac{1+x}{x}} = 1 \tag{5}\label{5}$$
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m this can be rearranged to:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m $$\left[\frac{\frac15\,,6}{5}\right]^x = 1 \tag{6}\label{6}$$
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m which gives us the fact :
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m $$\left(\frac{6}{5}\right)^x = 1+\quad \text{Which according to Eq.}\eqref{1}\eqref{3}\eqref{4}, \quad \text{becomes} \quad x = \frac{1}{\log \left(\frac{6}{5}\right)} \tag{7}\label{7}$$
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m This is the Y table:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m $$\begin{array}{ccc}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m x = x^{\frac{1}{x}} 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m &&&\text{(Eq. 7)}\\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m x^{\frac{1}{x}}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m & \to & 1+x+\cdots.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \end{array}\quad \text{from \eqref{2}}\quad \text{end} \quad \boxed[]{\text{becomes equal to }\,\eqref{1}}$$
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m which is the expected behavior as $\lim_{x\to+\infty}\left(\frac{5}{6}\right)^x=1$ and $\left(\frac{5}{6}\right)^{\infty}=1$.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m This shows that is also true for $n^\frac1n$ and $\left(\frac{5}{6}\right)^x$ for for larger $x$^2 therefore we get the following trend for : $n^{\frac{1}{n^2}} < 1 + 0.2071 n^{-1}$ For advice and race questions please see
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m [https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c6h534850p3130823]
