[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Find the remainder when $r^{13} + 1$ is divided by $r - 1$.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To find the remainder when \( r^{13} + 1 \) is divided by \( r - 1 \), we can use the Remainder Theorem. The Remainder Theorem states that if a polynomial \( f(r) \) is divided by \( r - r_0 \), the remainder is \( f(r_0) \).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m In this problem, our polynomial is \( f(r) = r^{13} + 1 \), and we need to divide by \( r - 1 \). Therefore, we need to find the remainder when \( r^{13} \) is divided by \( r - 1 \).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m According to the Remainder Theorem, the remainder when \( r^{13} \) is divided by \( r - 1 \) is simply \( r^{13} \) because:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ r^{13} \equiv 0 \pmod{r - 1} \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m This is because any positive integer \( r \) is less than 1 when expressed in modulo \( r - 1 \) which means \( r^{13} \) when divided by \( r - 1 \) will be equal to \( 0 \).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Therefore, the remainder when \( r^{13} + 1 \) is divided by \( r - 1 \) is:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ \boxed{0} \]<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
