[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m First, we know that 48 pages fit in a box and Winny's basket can hold at most 413 pages. Since 12 boxes can hold \(12 \times 48\) pages, the total number of pages she can read in a day is:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ 12 \times 48 = 576 \text{ pages} \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Next, we need to account for the boxes of 48 pages each that Winny will buy. Since she buys 12 more boxes, the total number of boxes she will have is:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ 12 + 12 = 24 \text{ boxes} \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Each box contains 48 pages, so the total number of pages she can read in 24 boxes is:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ 24 \times 48 = 1152 \text{ pages} \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Since the problem does not specify the reading rate or the rate at which she buys additional books, we assume she continues to read at the same rate as the question states. Therefore, the total number of pages Winny can buy before she needs to change inventory is:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ 1152 \text{ pages} \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
