[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - \( AB = 2 \) units
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - \( BC = 5 \) units
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - \( AD = 14 \) units
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - \( CD = x \) units for some unknown length \( x \)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Since \( AB \) and \( CD \) are on the same side of the line, we can express the lengths involving \( AB \) and \( CD \) in terms of the Unknown \( x \):
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ AB = 2 \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ CD = x \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Now, we use the fact that the sum of the segments on either side of the line will determine the lengths involved. Specifically:
