[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To find the integer \( n \) such that \( 0 \le n \le 180 \) and \(\cos n^\circ = \cos 259^\circ\), we need to recall a fundamental property of the cosine function. The function \(\cos \theta\) has a period of \(360^\circ\), meaning \(\cos \theta = \cos (\theta + 360^\circ k)\) for any integer \(k\). Therefore, \(\cos 259^\circ = \cos (259^\circ - 360^\circ k)\) for any integer \(k\).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Since we are asked to find \(n\) and \(n \le 180\), we need to determine when \(\cos n^\circ = \cos 259^\circ\). This means \(259^\circ - 360^\circ k = 259^\circ \pm 180^\circ\) for some integer \(k\).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Simplifying the equation, we get:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ 259^\circ - 360^\circ k = (259^\circ + 180^\circ) - 360^\circ k \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ 259^\circ - 360^\circ k = 439^\circ - 360^\circ k \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
