[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To find the phase shift of the graph of \( y = \sin (3x - \pi) \), we need to determine the shift introduced by the coefficient of \( x \) in the argument of the sine function. The general form for the sine function is \( y = \sin (ax + b) \), where \( a \) affects the period and \( b \) shifts the graph. Specifically, the phase shift is given by \( \frac{b}{a} \) when \( a > 0 \).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m In the given function \( y = \sin (3x - \pi) \), the amplitude \( a \) is 3 and the phase shift \( b \) is \(-\pi\). The phase shift is calculated as follows:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \text{Phase shift} = \frac{b}{a} = \frac{-\pi}{3}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Thus, the phase shift of the graph of \( y = \sin (3x - \pi) \) is \(\boxed{-\frac{\pi}{3}}\).<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
