[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To evaluate \([i^{14762} + i^{14763} + i^{14764} + i^{14765}]\), we start by recognizing that the powers of \(i\) cycle every four terms. Specifically, we have:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[i^1 = i, \quad i^2 = -1, \quad i^3 = -i, \quad i^4 = 1, \quad i^5 = i, \quad \text{and so on}.\]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m This cycle repeats every four terms. Therefore, we can simplify the exponents modulo 4:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[i^{14762} \equiv i^{14762 \mod 4} = i^{2} = -1, \quad i^{14763} \equiv i^{14763 \mod 4} = i^{3} = -i, \quad i^{14764} \equiv i^{14764 \mod 4} = i^{2} = -1, \quad i^{14765} \equiv i^{14765 \mod 4} = i^{1} = i.\]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Adding these together, we get:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[i^{14762} + i^{14763} + i^{14764} + i^{14765} \equiv -1 + (-i) + (-1) + i \equiv -1 - 1 - i + i \equiv -2 \equiv 2 \pmod{4}.\]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Since 2 is an even number, it is particularly interesting in the context of even numbers. Therefore, the final answer is:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[\boxed{2}.\]<|endoftext|>
