[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To find the value of an 8-foot stack of nickels, we need to follow these steps:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 1. **Convert the height of the stack from inches to feet:**
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    6.25 \text{ inches} = \frac{6.25}{12} \text{ feet} \approx 0.5208 \text{ feet}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 2. **Find the value of a single nickel in dollars:**
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    Since each nickel is worth $0.05 (as there are 5 cents in a dollar), the value of one nickel is:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    0.05 \text{ dollars}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 3. **Calculate the total value of the stack in dollars:**
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    The total value is the length of the stack multiplied by the value of a single nickel:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    0.5208 \text{ feet} \times 0.05 \text{ dollars/foot} \approx 0.02604 \text{ dollars}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 4. **Express the final answer to the nearest hundredth:**
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \boxed{0.03}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Thus, the value of an 8-foot stack of nickels is \(\boxed{0.03}\) dollars.<|endoftext|>
