[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To find the last two digits of the sum \(5! + 10! + 15! + \cdots + 100!\), we need to consider the last two digits of each factorial term and sum them up. Let's denote \( n! \) as the factorial of \( n \).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m The factorial function can be expanded as:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ n! = n \times (n-1) \times (n-2) \times \cdots \times 2 \times 1 \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m We need to determine the last two digits of each factorial from \( 5! \) to \( 100! \) and then sum them up.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m First, let's write out the sequence of factorials:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ 5! = 120 \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ 10! = 3628800 \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ 15! = 1307674368000 \]
