[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m How many values of $x$, $-19<x<98$, satisfy $\cos^2 x + 2\sin^2 x = 1?$  (Note: $x$ is measured in radians.)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To solve the equation \(\cos^2 x + 2\sin^2 x = 1\) for \(x\) in the interval \(-19 < x < 98\), we will use trigonometric identities and properties of sine and cosine.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m First, recall the Pythagorean identity:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \sin^2 x + \cos^2 x = 1
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m We can rearrange this identity to simplify our equation:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \frac{\sin^2 x}{\cos^2 x} + 1 = 1
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Subtracting 1 from both sides, we get:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \frac{\sin^2 x}{\cos^2 x} = 0
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m This implies:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \sin^2 x = 0
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Therefore, \(x\) must be of the form:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m x = k\pi, \quad k \in \mathbb{Z}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Within the interval \(-19 < x < 98\), the only integer values for \(x\) are:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m x = -19\pi, -18\pi, -17\pi, \ldots, 6\pi, 7\pi, 8\pi
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Since \(\sin x\) has a period of \(2\pi\) (i.e., \(\sin(x) = \sin(x + 2\pi)\)), we can reduce these values modulo \(2\pi\):
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m x = -19\pi \equiv 19\pi \pmod{2\pi}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m x = -18\pi \equiv -18\pi + 2\pi = -16\pi \equiv 0\pi \pmod{2\pi}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m x = -17\pi \equiv -17\pi + 2\pi = -15\pi \equiv -3\pi \equiv 4\pi \pmod{2\pi}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m x = -16\pi \equiv -16\pi + 2\pi = -14\pi \equiv -5\pi \equiv 6\pi \pmod{2\pi}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m x = -5\pi
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m x = 0\pi \equiv 0\pi \pmod{2\pi}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m x = 6\pi \equiv 6\pi - 2\pi = 4\pi \equiv 0\pi \pmod{2\pi}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m x = 7\pi \equiv 7\pi - 2\pi = 5\pi \equiv 0\pi \pmod{2\pi}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m x = 8\pi \equiv 8\pi - 2\pi = 6\pi \equiv 0\pi \pmod{2\pi}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m So the solutions within \(-19 < x < 98\) are:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m x = -19\pi, 0\pi, 6\pi, 7\pi, 8\pi
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Thus, the number of values of \(x\) in the given interval that satisfy the equation is:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \boxed{5}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m [Ground Truth]
