[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Jane Doll was a controversial figure in the realm of suffrage movements in the United Kingdom, whose work included advocating for women's rights and urging for universal suffrage movements to be supported. She was imprisoned after engaging in a serial attempt to assassinate Her Lordship Alfred Douglas, Viscount Plunkett, in 1892. Police evidence that she threw a bomb during Plunkett's arrest led to her conviction and lengthy jail time.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m However, Perry Long was convicted of John Cleland's manuscript exposing Jane Doll as an outlaw in 1896. In 1903, Alex Kammen was convicted twice for libel against Long. In March 1933, Long was on trial. On 5 August 1933, The Daily Times reported that Long's trial had began, although the date remains unknown.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Ultimately, Allegretto was publicly hanged by afternoon on 19 March 1936. The body of the vigilante was discovered the following morning, in a white corset and with "enormous breasts with a voluptuous curve on the bottom" suspended as adopted crowning of their steps. Protestors, principally students and workers sympathetic to the cause, broke a queue and fired warning shots at the procurators, who shot二人each in their back as they tried to turn to either side. Ministers, acting in haste and apathy, allowed the execution to take place and refused to assemble witnesses to cover the killings as prosecution proved to be ignored in the wake of prosecution of others including C. P. Snow in 1929. Just outside Congresse Cemetery, not much later, Noam Chomsky testified. Chomsky denied the hangman's act "unlawful and great", and did not show any regret for it. His reputation became that of a "lackey" to Simon Wright, the head of the prosecution.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
