[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Given an altitude of 4, what is a possible latitude and longitude?
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To find a possible latitude and longitude for an altitude of 4 on a sphere, we can use some basic trigonometry concepts when working in spherical coordinates. The altitude is the negative height of the object projected onto the surface of a sphere. The calculation would work in two rotations: the rotation of the sphere with the azimuthal angle around the z-axis (pitch), and the rotation with the elevation angle around the y-axis (Roll).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Given:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - Altitude is -4 (since the convex side of the azimuthal angle is below the octant axis, and the variable giving the azimuth has the same value as the z-coordinate in spherical coordinates, so -4/2 = -2)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - Pitch is 0 degrees (compass heading is straight up)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - Ye stands for the sine of the latitude. Since the radius of the sphere is 1, the radius of an arbitrary spherical triangle with vertices at (0, 0, 0) and (4, 0, 0), and using the half-angle formula for the sine function, we have:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ \sin^{-1}\left(\frac{R \cdot \sin(\text{altitude})}{2 \times D}\right) = \sin^{-1}\left(\frac{1 \cdot \sin(-2)}{2 \cdot 1}\right) = \sin^{-1}\left(\frac{-\sin(2)}{2}\right) = \frac{\pi}{3}. \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Thus, the latitude is:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ \text{Latitude} = \boldsymbol{\operatorname{atan2}}\left(\frac{-2}{1}\right) = -\frac{\pi}{3}A + \frac{2\pi}{3}, \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m where \(A\) is the angle from the xy-plane (measured in radians) and \(B\) is the angle from the y-axis.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m The longitude is then the same as the altitude:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ \text{Longitude} = \boldsymbol{\operatorname{atan2}}\left(\frac{4}{1}\right) = \boldsymbol{\operatorname{atan2}}(4), \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m where \(\text{atan2}\) returns the angle between the positive x-axis and the point \((x, y, z)\) in the counter-clockwise direction from the positive x-axis, with the x-axis being the y-axis
