[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To determine how many more ice cream cones Izzy can sell, we need to start by finding out how many more gallons of ice cream she can make with the remaining gallons of ice cream she has. 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m We know that an ice cream cone contains \(\frac{1}{32}\) of a gallon. Izzy has \(\frac{3}{4}\) of a gallon of ice cream left. Therefore, the amount of ice cream she can make with this remaining \(\frac{3}{4}\) of a gallon is:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \frac{3}{4} \div \frac{1}{32}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Dividing by a fraction is equivalent to multiplying by its reciprocal. The reciprocal of \(\frac{1}{32}\) is \(\frac{32}{1}\). So we have:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \frac{3}{4} \times \frac{32}{1} = \frac{3 \times 32}{4 \times 1} = \frac{96}{4} = 24
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m This means that the ice cream can be stretched or sold into 24 ice cream cones. To find out how many more ice cream cones she can sell, we subtract the number of ice cream cones already sold from the number of ice cream cones she can make with the remaining ice cream:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 24 - \frac{3}{4} = 24 - 0.75 = 23.25
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Since Izzy can only sell whole ice cream cones, we round down to the nearest whole number. Therefore, the number of additional ice cream cones she can sell is:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \boxed{23}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
