[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m A random sample of 40 adult men is selected from 5000 men in the United States. The sample mean weight of these 40 men is found to be 176.69 pounds. Calculate a 95% confidence interval for the true mean weight of all U.S. men.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To calculate a 95% confidence interval for the true mean weight of all U.S. men, we will use the following formula for the confidence interval of a sample mean when the population standard deviation is unknown:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \bar{x} \pm t_{\alpha/2, n-1} \left( \frac{s}{\sqrt{n}} \right)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m where:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - \(\bar{x}\) is the sample mean,
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - \(t_{\alpha/2, n-1}\) is the critical value from the t-distribution with \(n-1\) degrees of freedom,
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - \(s\) is the sample standard deviation,
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - \(n\) is the sample size.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Given:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - \(\bar{x} = 176.69\) pounds,
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - \(s = 11.2\) pounds,
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - \(n = 40\),
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - The confidence level is 95%, so \(\alpha = 0.05\).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m First, we need to find the critical value \(t_{\alpha/2, n-1}\). Since \(\alpha = 0.05\) and \(n-1 = 39\), we have \(\alpha/2 = 0.025\). From the t-table, \(t_{0.025, 39} \approx 2.024\).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Next, we calculate the standard error of the mean:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \text{Standard Error} = \frac{s}{\sqrt{n}} = \frac{11.2}{\sqrt{40}} \approx \frac{11.2}{6.3246} \approx 1.85
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Now, we can find the margin of error:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \text{Margin of Error} = t_{\alpha/2, n-1} \left( \frac{s}{\sqrt{n}} \right) = 2.024 \times 1.85 \approx 3.77
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Finally, we construct the 95% confidence interval:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \bar{x} \pm \text{Margin of Error} = 176.69 \pm 3.77
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m This gives us the confidence interval:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m (176.69 - 3.77, 176.69 + 3.77) = (172.92, 170.46)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Therefore, the 95% confidence interval for the true mean weight of all U.S. men is \(\boxed{(172.92, 170.46)}\).<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
