[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m An isosceles right triangle is removed from each corner of a square piece of paper, as shown, to create a rectangle. If $AB = 12$ units, what is the combined area of the four removed triangles, in square units? [asy]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m unitsize(5mm);
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m defaultpen(linewidth(.7pt)+fontsize(8pt));
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m pair A=(1,4), Ap=(0,3), B=(3,0), Bp=(4,1);
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m draw((0,0)--(0,4)--(4,4)--(4,0)--cycle);
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m draw(A--Ap--B--Bp--cycle,linetype("4 3"));
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m label("$A$",A,N);
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m label("$B$",B,S);
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m [/asy]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To find the combined area of the four removed triangles, we need to calculate the area of the original square piece of paper and subtract the area of the four small triangles that are formed by removing each corner.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 1. Determine the side length of the original square:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    Since the area of the original square is \(AB = 12\), the side length \(s\) is:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    s = \sqrt{12^2} = 12
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 2. Calculate the area of the square:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \text{Area of the square} = s^2 = 12^2 = 144 \text{ square units}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 3. Determine the area of one of the triangles:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    Each triangle is an isosceles right triangle and has legs equal to half the length of the side of the square. The legs of the isosceles right triangle are each:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \text{leg length} = \frac{s}{2} = \frac{12}{2} = 6 \text{ units}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    The area of one of these right triangles is:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \text{Area of one triangle} = \frac{1}{2} \times \text{leg length} \times \text{leg length} = \frac{1}{2} \times 6 \times 6 = 18 \text{ square units}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 4. Determine the combined area of the four triangles:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    Since there are four such triangles, the total area of the four triangles is:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \text{Total area of the four triangles} = 4 \times 18 = 72 \text{ square units}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Therefore, the combined area of the four removed triangles is \(\boxed{72}\).<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
