[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Two electrons are tied together by the same primitive magnetic field {eq}\vec B
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m {/eq}, which has a magnitude of 10.50 T, directed in the positive x-direction. They move left to right beneath a uniform magnetic field {eq}\vec B = B_o \hat i
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m {/eq} traveling in the positive z-direction. The speed of both electrons is 3.20 m/s. What is the speed of the electrons measured in the region of higher magnetic field? Give your answer with two significant figures. To determine the speed of the electrons measured in the region of higher magnetic field, we need to analyze the motion of the electron system under the influence of both magnetic and electric forces.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m ### Step 1: Consider the magnetic force on each electron
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m The magnetic force on an electron moving with velocity \(\vec{v}\) in the direction of the magnetic field \(\vec{B}\) is given by:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \vec{F}_B = q \vec{v} \times \vec{B}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m where \(q\) is the charge of the electron and \(\vec{v}\) is the velocity of the electron.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Given:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - Charge of an electron, \(q = 1.602 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C}\)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - Magnitude of the magnetic field, \(B = 10.50 \, \text{T}\)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - Magnitude of the velocity of both electrons, \(v = 3.20 \, \text{m/s}\)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - Direction of the magnetic field is in the positive x-direction
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m ### Step 2: Calculate the magnetic force on the electron
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m The magnetic force on the electron is:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \vec{F}_B = (1.602 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C}) (3.20 \, \text{m/s}) \times (10.50 \, \text{T}) \hat i
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \vec{F}_B = (1.602 \times 10^{-19}) (3.20) (10.50) \hat i
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \vec{F}_B = (5.1236 \times 10^{-19}) \hat i
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m ### Step 3: Neglecting the electric force for the low magnetic field case
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Since the electron's charge is large compared to the magnetic field, the electric force on the electron is negligible. Therefore, the magnetic force is the sole force acting on the electron.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m ### Step 4: Determine the speed of the electrons in the region of the higher magnetic field
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m In the region of the higher magnetic field, the electron experiences a net force due to the magnetic force alone, and the electric force is zero (since the electric field is not in the same direction).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Using Newton's second law:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m F = ma
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m where \(F\) is the magnetic force, \(m\) is the mass of the electron, and \(a\) is the acceleration of the electron.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Using the formula for the acceleration of a moving charge:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m a = \frac{F}{m}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m This is the same as the magnetic force:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m a = \frac{(5.1236 \times 10^{-19}) m}{m}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m a = 5.1236 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{m/s}^2
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m The electron's speed \(v\) can be found using:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m a = \frac{dm}{dt}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m From newton's second law for acceleration:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m m\frac{dv}{dt} = F
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Given \(m = qV\), where \(V\) is the electron velocity in the region of the higher magnetic field.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m For a neutrino (sans electron), this simplifies to:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m v_{\text{neutrino}} = v_{\text{neut.}} = v_{\text{neutron}} = v_{\text{multiplet}} = v
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m ### Step 5: Calculate the electron speed
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m The speed of the electron in the region of higher magnetic field is:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m v = \frac{(5.1236 \times 10^{-19}) m}{m} = 5.1236 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{m/s}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Using the mass of an electron \(m = 9.109 \times 10^{-31} \, \text{kg}\):
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m v = \frac{(5.1236 \times 10^{-19}) (9.109 \times 10^{-31})}{9.109 \times 10^{-31}}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m v = \frac{5.1236 \times 9.109 \times 10^{-50}}{9.109 \times 10^{-31}}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m v = 5.1236 \times 9.109 \times 10^{-20}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m v \approx 45.25 \times 10^{-20}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m v \approx 4.53 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{m/s}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m ### Final Answer
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m The speed of the electrons measured in the region of higher magnetic field is:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \boxed{4.5 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{m/s}}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m What are the general solutions to the trigonometric equation:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m {eq}5\sin^2 \theta = \cos \theta {/eq} To solve the trigonometric equation \(5\sin^2 \theta = \cos \theta\), we will follow a systematic approach. Let's start by using the Pythagorean identity \(\sin^2 \theta + \cos^2 \theta = 1\).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m First, we rewrite \(\sin^2 \theta\) in terms of \(\cos \theta\):
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \sin^2 \theta = \frac{1 - \cos^2 \theta}{2}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Substitute this into the original equation:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 5 \left(\frac{1 - \cos^2 \theta}{2}\right) = \cos \theta
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Simplify the left-hand side:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \frac{5(1 - \cos^2 \theta)}{2} = \cos \theta
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Multiply both sides by 2 to clear the fraction:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 5(1 - \cos^2 \theta) = 2 \cos \theta
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Distribute the 5:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 5 - 5\cos^2 \theta = 2\cos \theta
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Rearrange the equation to isolate \(\cos^2 \theta\):
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 5 - 2\cos \theta = 5\cos^2 \theta
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Multiply both sides by 5 to clear the fraction:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 5 \cdot 5 - 2 \cdot 5\cos \theta = 5 \cdot 5\cos^2 \theta
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 25 - 10\cos \theta = 25\cos^2 \theta
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Rearrange into a standard quadratic equation form:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 25\cos^2 \theta + 10\cos \theta - 25 = 0
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Divide the entire equation by 25 to simplify:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \cos^2 \theta + \frac{2}{5}\cos \theta - 1 = 0
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Let \(u = \cos \theta\). Then the equation becomes:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m u^2 + \frac{2}{5}u - 1 = 0
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m This is a standard quadratic equation of the form \(au^2 + bu + c = 0\), where \(a = 1\), \(b = \frac{2}{5}\), and \(c = -1\). We solve this using the quadratic formula \(u = \frac{-b \pm \sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\):
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m u = \frac{-\frac{2}{5} \pm \sqrt{\left(\frac{2}{5}\right)^2 - 4 \cdot 1 \cdot (-1)}}{2 \cdot 1}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m u = \frac{-\frac{2}{5} \pm \sqrt{\frac{4}{25} + 4}}{2}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m u = \frac{-\frac{2}{5} \pm \sqrt{\frac{4}{25} + \frac{100}{25}}}{2}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m u = \frac{-\frac{2}{5} \pm \sqrt{\frac{104}{25}}}{2}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m u = \frac{-\frac{2}{5} \pm \frac{2\sqrt{26}}{5}}{2}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m u = \frac{-1 \pm \sqrt{26}}{5}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m So, we have two solutions for \(\cos \theta\):
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \cos \theta = \frac{-1 + \sqrt{26}}{5} \quad \text{or} \quad \cos \theta = \frac{-1 - \sqrt{26}}{5}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Next, we find the corresponding values of \(\theta\). For \(\cos \theta = \frac{-1 + \sqrt{26}}{5}\):
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \theta = \cos^{-1} \left( \frac{-1 + \sqrt{26}}{5} \right) \quad \text{or} \quad \theta = 2\pi - \cos^{-1} \left( \frac{-1 + \sqrt{26}}{5} \right)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m For \(\cos \theta = \frac{-1 - \sqrt{26}}{5}\):
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \theta = \cos^{-1} \left( \frac{-1 - \sqrt{26}}{5} \right) \quad \text{or} \quad \theta = 2\pi - \cos^{-1} \left( \frac{-1 - \sqrt{26}}{5} \right)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Thus, the general solutions to the trigonometric equation \(5\sin^2 \theta = \cos \theta\) are:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \boxed{\theta = \cos^{-1} \left( \frac{-1 + \sqrt{26}}{5} \right) + 2k\pi \quad \text{or} \quad \theta = 2\pi - \cos^{-1} \left( \frac{-1 + \sqrt{26}}{5} \right) + 2k\pi \quad \text{or} \quad \theta = \cos^{-1} \left( \frac{-1 - \sqrt{26}}{5} \right) + 2k\pi \quad \text{or} \quad \theta = 2\pi - \cos^{-1} \left( \frac{-1 - \sqrt{26}}{5} \right) + 2k\pi}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m ****
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m A rectangular block has a width and length of 0.21 m and a height of 0.10 m. Find the volume of the block.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m **
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To find the volume of the rectangular block, we use the formula for the volume of a rectangular prism, which is given by:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ \text{Volume} = \text{length} \times \text{width} \times \text{height} \]
