[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Find the sum of the infinite series $1+2\left(\dfrac{1}{1998}\right)+3\left(\dfrac{1}{1998}\right)^2+4\left(\dfrac{1}{1998}\right)^3+\cdots$.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To find the sum of the infinite series \(1 + 2\left(\dfrac{1}{1998}\right) + 3\left(\dfrac{1}{1998}\right)^2 + 4\left(\dfrac{1}{1998}\right)^3 + \cdots\), we can recognize that this is a case of an arithmetico-geometric series.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m This series can be written as:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ S = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} n \left(\dfrac{1}{1998}\right)^{n-1} \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m We need to find the sum \( S \) of this series. To do this, we will use the formula for the sum of an arithmetico-geometric series. If \( S \) is the sum of the series \( \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} n x^{n-1} \), then:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ S = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} n \cdot x^{n-1} = \frac{x}{(1-x)^2} \quad \text{for} \quad |x| < 1 \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Here, we can let \( x = \dfrac{1}{1998} \). So, we have:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ S = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} n \left(\dfrac{1}{1998}\right)^{n-1} = \frac{\dfrac{1}{1998}}{\left(1 - \frac{1}{1998}\right)^2} = \frac{\frac{1}{1998}}{\left(\frac{1997}{1998}\right)^2} = \frac{\frac{1}{1998}}{\frac{1997^2}{1998^2}} = \frac{1998^2}{1998 \cdot 1997^2} = \frac{1}{1997^2} \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Therefore, the sum of the infinite series is:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ \boxed{\frac{1}{1997^2}} \]<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m [Ground Truth]
