[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To find the least possible product of two distinct primes that are each greater than 20, we need to identify the smallest primes greater than 20. The smallest prime number greater than 20 is 23.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m We will check if the product of 23 and 29 is the minimum product. 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m First, calculate the product of 23 and 29:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ 23 \times 29 \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Using the distributive property:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ 23 \times 29 = 23 \times (30 - 1) = 23 \times 30 - 23 \times 1 \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Now, compute each term separately:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ 23 \times 30 = 690 \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ 23 \times 1 = 23 \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Subtract the second term from the first:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ 690 - 23 = 667 \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Thus, the least possible product of 23 and 29 is 667.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Therefore, the least possible product of two distinct primes greater than 20 is:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \boxed{667}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
