[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To find the polar form of the complex number \((3 \operatorname{cis} 18^\circ)(-2 \operatorname{cis} 37^\circ)\), we can use the properties of complex numbers in polar form. For two complex numbers \(z_1 = r_1 \operatorname{cis} \theta_1\) and \(z_2 = r_2 \operatorname{cis} \theta_2\), their product is given by:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m z_1 \cdot z_2 = r_1 r_2 \operatorname{cis} (\theta_1 + \theta_2)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Here, \(z_1 = 3 \operatorname{cis} 18^\circ\) and \(z_2 = -2 \operatorname{cis} 37^\circ\). So, we need to calculate \(r_1 r_2\) and \(\theta_1 + \theta_2\).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m First, compute \(r_1 r_2\):
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
