[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To find the oblique asymptote of the fraction $\frac{2x^2 + 7x + 10}{2x + 3}$, we need to perform polynomial long division of the numerator by the denominator.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 1. **Set up the division:**
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \begin{array}{r|rr}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m      2x + 3 & 2x^2 + 7x + 10 \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \end{array}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 2. **Divide the leading term of the dividend by the leading term of the divisor:**
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \frac{2x^2}{2x} = x
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    So, the first term of the quotient is $x$.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 3. **Multiply the entire divisor by this term and subtract from the dividend:**
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \begin{array}{r|rr}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m      2x + 3 & 2x^2 + 7x + 10 \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m            & x \cdot (2x + 3) \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \hline
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m            & 2x^2 + 7x + 10 \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m            & - (2x + 3) \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \end{array}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    Simplify the subtraction:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \begin{array}{r|rr}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m      2x + 3 & 2x^2 + 7x + 10 \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m            & x \cdot (2x + 3) \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m            & 2x^2 + 7x + 10 \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m            & -(2x + 3) \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \end{array}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    This gives us:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \begin{array}{r|rr}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m      2x + 3 & 2x^2 + 7x + 10 \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m        & x \cdot (2x + 3) \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m        & 2x^2 + 7x + 10 \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m        & -(2x + 3) \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \end{array}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    After performing the division, we see that the remainder is 7.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 4. **Write the final quotient and remainder:**
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \frac{2x^2 + 7x + 10}{2x + 3} = x + \frac{7}{2x + 3} + \frac{7}{(2x + 3)^2}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    The remainder is 7, which means the oblique asymptote is $y = x + \frac{7}{2x + 3} + \frac{7}{(2x + 3)^2}$.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To put the equation in the form $y = mx + b$, we separate the terms and combine the constants:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m y = 2x - \frac{7}{2x + 3} + \frac{7}{(2x + 3)^2}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Thus, the equation of the oblique asymptote is:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \boxed{y = 2x - \frac{7}{2x + 3} + \frac{7}{(2x + 3)^2}}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
