[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To find the value of \(2 \diamondsuit 7\) given the operation \(x \diamondsuit y = 3x + 5y\) for all \(x\) and \(y\), we can substitute \(x = 2\) and \(y = 7\) into the given operation formula.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m The operation is defined as:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ x \diamondsuit y = 3x + 5y \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Substituting \(x = 2\) and \(y = 7\), we get:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ 2 \diamondsuit 7 = 3(2) + 5(7) \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Now, we perform the multiplication:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ 3(2) = 6 \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ 5(7) = 35 \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m So, combining these results, we have:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ 2 \diamondsuit 7 = 6 + 35 = 41 \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Therefore, the value of \(2 \diamondsuit 7\) is \(\boxed{41}\).<|endoftext|>
