[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To find the remainder when \(8 \cdot 10^{18} + 1^{18}\) is divided by 9, we will simplify each part of the expression modulo 9.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m First, consider \(8 \cdot 10^{18} \mod 9\). We can use the property of exponents that \(10 \equiv 1 \mod 9\). Therefore, \(10^{18} \equiv 1^{18} \equiv 1 \mod 9\). This means:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[8 \cdot 10^{18} \equiv 8 \cdot 1 \equiv 8 \mod 9.\]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Since \(8 \equiv 8 \mod 9\), we have:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[8 \cdot 10^{18} \equiv 8 \mod 9.\]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Next, consider \(1^{18} \mod 9\). Again, using the property of exponents, \(1^{18} = 1\). Therefore:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[1^{18} \equiv 1 \mod 9.\]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Since \(1 \equiv 1 \mod 9\), we have:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[1^{18} \equiv 1 \mod 9.\]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Now, we combine the results from both parts modulo 9:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[8 \cdot 10^{18} + 1^{18} \equiv 8 + 1 \equiv 9 \mod 9.\]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Since \(9 \equiv 0 \mod 9\), the remainder when \(8 \cdot 10^{18} + 1^{18}\) is divided by 9 is:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[\boxed{0}.\]<|endoftext|>
