[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    The repeating sequence is "144444".
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 3. **Determine the position of the 100th digit:**
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    - Since the decimal repeats every 1 digits, we need to find the position within one complete cycle.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    - The 100th digit corresponds to the 10th digit in the repeating sequence.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 4. **Count the digits:**
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    - The sequence "144444" has 3 digits.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    - Therefore, the 10th digit in the sequence is the 10th digit itself.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 5. **Extract the digit:**
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    - The digit at position 9 of the repeating sequence "144444" is 4.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Thus, the 100th digit to the right of the decimal point in the decimal representation of \(\frac{13}{90}\) is \(\boxed{4}\).<|endoftext|>
