[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m When we substitute $\pi/4$ into the tangent function, we get $\tan(\pi/4) = 1$.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Let's verify this using Python's `sympy` library: We can directly compute the tangent of 135 degrees (or $\frac{3\pi}{4}$ radians).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Here's the Python code to compute this using `sympy`.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m ```python
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m import sympy as sp
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m # Define the angle in degrees
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m angle = sp.Rational(3, 4) * sp.pi
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m # Compute the tangent of the angle
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m tan_value = sp.tan(angle)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m # Print the result
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m print(tan_value)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m ```
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m ```output
