[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To find \(\sec 135^\circ\), we need to understand the definition of the secant function. The secant of an angle is the reciprocal of the cosine of that angle. So, \(\sec \theta = \frac{1}{\cos \theta}\).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m First, we need to determine \(\cos 135^\circ\). The angle \(135^\circ\) is in the second quadrant, and the reference angle for \(135^\circ\) is \(180^\circ - 135^\circ = 45^\circ\). The cosine of the reference angle \(45^\circ\) is \(\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} = \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m In the second quadrant, the cosine of an angle is negative, so \(\cos 135^\circ = -\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Now, we can find \(\sec 135^\circ\):
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \sec 135^\circ = \frac{1}{\cos 135^\circ} = \frac{1}{-\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}} = -\frac{2}{\sqrt{2}} = \frac{-2}{\sqrt{2} \cdot \sqrt{2}} = \frac{-2}{2} = -1
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Therefore, the value of \(\sec 135^\circ\) is \(\boxed{-1}\).<|endoftext|>
