[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Lee can make 18 cookies with two cups of flour. How many cookies can he make with three cups of flour?
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To determine how many cookies Lee can make with three cups of flour, we start by understanding the ratio of cookies made with flour to cups of flour.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m We know that 2 cups of flour can make 18 cookies. Therefore, the ratio of cookies to cups of flour is:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ \text{Cookies per cup of flour} = \frac{18}{2} = 9 \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Now, we need to find out how many cookies (denoted as \( x \)) can be made with 3 cups of flour. We can set up the following proportion based on the ratio:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ \frac{\text{Cookies}}{\text{Cups of flour}} = \frac{x}{3} \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Substituting the ratio we know (9 cookies per cup of flour) in the proportion, we get:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ \frac{x}{3} = 9 \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To solve for \( x \), we can multiply both sides of the equation by 3:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ x = 9 \times 3 \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ x = 27 \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m So, Lee can make \(\boxed{27}\) cookies with three cups of flour.<|endoftext|>
