[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Moe is trying to decide whether to forage for mushrooms or buy them at the store. A single mushroom has a probability of 0.5 to be poisonous. If Moe picks a random number at random, he eats it. Compute the expected number of mushrooms he will eat if he pranks 100 colleagues and donates 95 to them.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To solve this problem, we need to consider the outcome of each lottery that Moe participates in. Moe participates in a one-sided lottery where each person has a 50% chance of being poisonous and a 50% chance of being non-poisonous. If a person is chosen due to a non-poisonous result, Moe eats the mushroom; if it is non-poisonous, he doesn't eat the mushroom.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m If Moe picks 100 volunteers at random through the process described, the expected number of poisoned mushrooms picked is:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ 100 \cdot 0.5 = 50. \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m This means that out of the 100 volunteers, on average, 50 are non-poisonous. Therefore, on average, 50 mushrooms will not be eaten, so the expected number of mushrooms that Moe will eat is still 50.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Thus, the expected number of mushrooms Moe will eat if he pranks 100 colleagues and donates 95 to them is:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \boxed{50}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
