[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To find the last three digits of \(3^{12000}\), we need to determine \(3^{12000} \mod 1000\). We are given that \(3^{400} \equiv 1 \pmod{1000}\), and this means that the order of 3 modulo 1000 is 400. This tells us that \(3^{12000} = (3^{400})^{30} \cdot 3^{800} \equiv 3^{800} \pmod{1000}\).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Given that \(3^{400} \equiv 1 \pmod{1000}\), we can substitute to get:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[3^{800} \equiv 3 \cdot 3^{600} \equiv 3 \cdot (3^{400})^{150} \equiv 3 \cdot 1 \equiv 3 \pmod{1000}.\]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Therefore, the last three digits of \(3^{12000}\) are \(\boxed{03}\).<|endoftext|>
