[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To find the area of \(\triangle ABC\), we will use the formula for the area of a triangle given its vertices. The vertices are \((x_1, y_1) = (4, 9)\), \((x_2, y_2) = (0, 0)\), and \((x_3, y_3) = (12, 0)\).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m The area \(A\) of a triangle with vertices \((x_1, y_1)\), \((x_2, y_2)\), and \((x_3, y_3)\) can be calculated using the following formula:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m A = \frac{1}{2} \left| x_1(y_2 - y_3) + x_2(y_3 - y_1) + x_3(y_1 - y_2) \right|
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Substituting the coordinates of the vertices \((4, 9)\), \((0, 0)\), and \((12, 0)\) into the formula, we get:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m A = \frac{1}{2} \left| 4(0 - 0) + 0(0 - 9) + 12(9 - 0) \right|
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m A = \frac{1}{2} \left| 0 + 0 + 12 \cdot 9 \right|
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m A = \frac{1}{2} \left| 108 \right|
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m A = \frac{1}{2} \cdot 108
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m A = 54
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Therefore, the area of \(\triangle ABC\) is \(\boxed{54}\).<|endoftext|>
