[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To find the midpoint of a segment with endpoints \((7, -6)\) and \((-3, 4)\), we use the midpoint formula. The midpoint \((M)\) of a line segment with endpoints \((x_1, y_1)\) and \((x_2, y_2)\) is given by:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m M = \left( \frac{x_1 + x_2}{2}, \frac{y_1 + y_2}{2} \right)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Here are the steps to calculate the midpoint:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 1. Identify the x-coordinates and y-coordinates of the endpoints:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    - \(x_1 = 7\), \(y_1 = -6\)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    - \(x_2 = -3\), \(y_2 = 4\)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 2. Substitute these values into the midpoint formula:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    M = \left( \frac{7 + (-3)}{2}, \frac{-6 + 4}{2} \right)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 3. Simplify the expressions inside the parentheses:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    M = \left( \frac{7 - 3}{2}, \frac{-6 + 4}{2} \right) = \left( \frac{4}{2}, \frac{-2}{2} \right)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 4. Perform the division:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    M = (2, -1)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Therefore, the midpoint of the segment with endpoints \((7, -6)\) and \((-3, 4)\) is \(\boxed{(2, -1)}\).<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
