[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m The Lucas sequence is the sequence 1, 3, 4, 7, 11, $\ldots$ where the first term is 1, the second term is 3 and each term after that is the sum of the previous two terms. What is the remainder when the $100^{\mathrm{th}}$ term of the sequence is divided by 8?
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To find the \(100^{\text{th}}\) term of the Lucas sequence and the remainder when it is divided by 8, we start by understanding the sequence and the properties of the terms modulo 8.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m The Lucas sequence is defined as follows:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - The first term \(L_1 = 1\).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - The second term \(L_2 = 3\).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - For \(n \geq 3\), the \(n\)-th term \(L_n\) is the sum of the (n-1)-th and (n-2)-th terms:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m   \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m   L_n = L_{n-1} + L_{n-2}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m   \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m We can compute the first few terms of the Lucas sequence modulo 8:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \begin{aligned}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m L_1 &= 1, \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m L_2 &= 3, \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m L_3 &= L_2 + L_1 = 3 + 1 = 4, \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m L_4 &= L_3 + L_2 = 4 + 3 = 7, \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m L_5 &= L_4 + L_3 = 7 + 4 = 11, \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m L_6 &= L_5 + L_4 = 11 + 7 = 18 \equiv 2 \pmod{8}, \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m L_7 &= L_6 + L_5 = 2 + 11 = 13 \equiv 5 \pmod{8}, \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m L_8 &= L_7 + L_6 = 5 + 2 = 7, \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m L_9 &= L_8 + L_7 = 7 + 5 = 12 \equiv 4 \pmod{8}, \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m L_{10} &= L_9 + L_8 = 4 + 7 = 11 \equiv 3 \pmod{8}, \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m L_{11} &= L_{10} + L_9 = 3 + 4 = 7, \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m L_{12} &= L_{11} + L_{10} = 7 + 3 = 10 \equiv 2 \pmod{8}, \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m L_{13} &= L_{12} + L_{11} = 2 + 7 = 9 \equiv 1 \pmod{8}, \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m L_{14} &= L_{13} + L_{12} = 1 + 2 = 3 \pmod{8}, \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m L_{15} &= L_{14} + L_{13} = 3 + 1 = 4 \pmod{8}, \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m &\vdots
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \end{aligned}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m We observe that the sequence modulo 8 repeats every 20 values. Specifically, the sequence modulo 8 is:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 1, 3, 4, 7, 2, 5, 7, 3, 1, 4, 2, 5, 7, 3, 1, \ldots
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To find the 100th term, we determine the position of the 100th term within the repeating block:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 100 \div 20 = 5 \text{ remainder } 0
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m This means the 100th term is the same as the 5th term in the repeating block, which is 1.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Thus, the 100th term of the Lucas sequence is 1, and the remainder when it is divided by 8 is:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \boxed{1}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m [Ground Truth]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 7
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m [Is Boxed Matched]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m True
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
