[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To expand the expression \((2x + 3)(x + 5)\), we will use the distributive property, which states that \(a(b + c) = ab + ac\).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Here, we have:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m (2x + 3)(x + 5)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m First, distribute \(2x\) to both \(x\) and \(5\):
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 2x \cdot x + 2x \cdot 5 = 2x^2 + 10x
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Next, distribute \(3\) to both \(x\) and \(5\):
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 3 \cdot x + 3 \cdot 5 = 3x + 15
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Now, combine the results from the two distributions:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 2x^2 + 10x + 3x + 15
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Combine the like terms \(10x\) and \(3x\):
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 2x^2 + 13x + 15
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Thus, the expanded form of \((2x + 3)(x + 5)\) is:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \boxed{2x^2 + 13x + 15}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
