[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To determine how many hours 36 bales of hay will last for 12 horses, we need to follow these steps:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 1. **Find the rate at which one horse eats hay:**
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    We know that six horses eat 12 bales of hay in 12 hours. Therefore, the total amount of hay eaten by one horse is:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \frac{12 \text{ bales}}{12 \text{ hours}} = 1 \text{ bale per hour}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 2. **Determine the rate at which 12 horses eat hay:**
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    Since one horse eats 1 bale of hay per hour, 12 horses will eat:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    12 \text{ horses} \times 1 \text{ bale per horse per hour} = 12 \text{ bales per hour}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 3. **Calculate the time it takes for 36 bales to be eaten by 12 horses:**
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    To find out how many hours it takes for 36 bales of hay to be eaten at the rate of 12 bales per hour, we use the following calculation:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \frac{36 \text{ bales}}{12 \text{ bales per hour}} = 3 \text{ hours}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Therefore, 36 bales of hay will last \boxed{3} hours for 12 horses.<|endoftext|>
