[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To determine the area of triangle \(\triangle COB\), we start by identifying the coordinates of the points \(C\), \(O\), and \(B\). Given that \(C\) is at \((0, p)\) and \(B\) is at \((12, 0)\), and \(O\) is at \((0, 0)\), the height from \(O\) to line segment \(CB\) is 12 units (since \(B\) is directly above \(O\)), and the base is the horizontal distance from \(O\) to \(C\), which is 12 units.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m The area \(A\) of a triangle is given by the formula:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m A = \frac{1}{2} \times \text{base} \times \text{height}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Here, the base is 12 units and the height is 12 units, so:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m A = \frac{1}{2} \times 12 \times 12 = \frac{1}{2} \times 144 = 72
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Therefore, the area of \(\triangle COB\) is \(\boxed{72}\).<|endoftext|>
