[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 2. **Reduce 256 modulo 5:**
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    256 \div 5 = 51 \text{ remainder } 1
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    So, \(256 \equiv 1 \pmod{5}\).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 3. **Generalize the pattern:**
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    We observe that each power of 2, when divided by 5, leaves a remainder of 1. This is because:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    2^1 = 2 \equiv 2 \pmod{5}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    2^2 = 4 \equiv -1 \pmod{5}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    2^3 = 8 \equiv -3 \pmod{5}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    2^4 = 16 \equiv -3 \equiv 2 \pmod{5}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    and so on.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 4. **Apply the pattern to \(2^8\):**
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    Since \(2^8\) is the 8th power of 2, and we have already shown that \(2^8 \equiv 1 \pmod{5}\), we conclude:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    2^8 \equiv 1 \pmod{5}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Therefore, the remainder when \(2^8\) is divided by 5 is \(\boxed{1}\).<|endoftext|>
