[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To compute \((\cos 185^\circ + i \sin 185^\circ)^{54}\), we will use De Moivre's Theorem. De Moivre's Theorem states that for any complex number in polar form \(r(\cos \theta + i \sin \theta)\) and any integer \(n\),
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m (r(\cos \theta + i \sin \theta))^n = r^n (\cos (n\theta) + i \sin (n\theta)).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m First, let's identify the magnitude and argument of the complex number \(\cos 185^\circ + i \sin 185^\circ\). The magnitude \(r\) is 1 and the argument \(\theta\) is \(185^\circ\). Therefore, we can write:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \cos 185^\circ + i \sin 185^\circ = \cos 185^\circ + i \sin 185^\circ = \text{cis } 185^\circ,
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m where \(\text{cis } \theta = \cos \theta + i \sin \theta\).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Now, applying De Moivre's Theorem with \(n = 54\), we get:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m (\text{cis } 185^\circ)^{54} = \text{cis } (185^\circ \cdot 54).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Next, we need to compute the argument of the resulting complex number. The argument of \(\text{cis } \theta\) is \(\theta\), so we have:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 185^\circ \cdot 54 = 10130^\circ.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To simplify \(10130^\circ\) modulo \(360^\circ\), we note that \(10130^\circ = 5 \cdot 360^\circ + 130^\circ\). Therefore,
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 10130^\circ \equiv 130^\circ \pmod{360^\circ}.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m So, we can write:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \text{cis } 10130^\circ = \cos 130^\circ + i \sin 130^\circ.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Thus, the final answer is:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m (\cos 185^\circ + i \sin 185^\circ)^{54} = \cos 130^\circ + i \sin 130^\circ.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Putting the final answer in a box, we get:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
