[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To find the remainder when \(3y^4 - 4y^3 + 5y^2 - 13y + 4\) is divided by \(3y - 2\), we can use polynomial long division. Here's the step-by-step process:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 1. **Set up the division**:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m   	\[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \begin{array}{r|rrrrr}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m      3y - 2 & 3y^4 & -4y^3 & +5y^2 & -13y & +4 \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \end{array}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 2. **Divide the leading term of the dividend by the leading term of the divisor**:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \frac{3y^4}{3y} = y^3
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    Write \(y^3\) above the division bar:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \begin{array}{r|rrrrr}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m      3y - 2 & 3y^4 & -4y^3 & +5y^2 & -13y & +4 \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m            & y^3 \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \end{array}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 3. **Multiply the entire divisor by the leading term of the dividend** and subtract from the dividend:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    (3y - 2) \cdot y^3 = 3y^4 - 2y^3
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    Subtract:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \begin{array}{r|rrrrr}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m      3y - 2 & 3y^4 & -4y^3 & +5y^2 & -13y & +4 \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m            & y^3 \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m     -(3y^4 - 2y^3) \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m            & & -2y^3 \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \end{array}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 4. **Repeat the process with the new polynomial**:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \frac{-2y^3}{3y} = -\frac{2}{3}y^2
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    Write \(-\frac{2}{3}y^2\) above the division bar:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \begin{array}{r|rrrrr}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m      3y - 2 & 3y^4 & -4y^3 & +5y^2 & -13y & +4 \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m            & y^3 \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m     -\frac{2}{3}y^2 \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m     \hline
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m        & & -\frac{2}{3}y^2 \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \end{array}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 5. **Multiply the entire divisor by the term added in the previous step and subtract**:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    (3y - 2) \cdot -\frac{2}{3}y^2 = -2y^3 + \frac{4}{3}y^2
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    Subtract:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \begin{array}{r|rrrrr}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m      3y - 2 & 3y^4 & -4y^3 & +5y^2 & -13y & +4 \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m            & y^3 \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m     -(3y^4 - 2y^3) \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m            & & -2y^3 \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m     & -\frac{2}{3}y^2 \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m            & +\frac{4}{3}y^2 \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \end{array}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 6. **Continue the process until the degree of the remainder is less than the degree of the divisor**:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \begin{array}{r|rrrrr}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m      3y - 2 & 3y^4 & -4y^3 & +5y^2 & -13y & +4 \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m            & y^3 \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m     -\frac{2}{3}y^2 \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m     -\frac{2}{3}y^2 \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m     & -2y^3 \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \end{array}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 7. **The degree of the remainder is less than the degree of the divisor**:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \boxed{1}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Therefore, the remainder when \(3y^4 - 4y^3 + 5y^2 - 13y + 4\) is divided by \(3y - 2\) is \(\boxed{1}\).<|endoftext|>
