[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m      \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    - As \( x \) approaches \( \infty \) or \( -\infty \):
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m      \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m      \text{As } x \to \infty \text{ or } -\infty , \frac{2x + 7}{x - 3} \to 0 \quad \text{since the numerator grows or decays at different rates}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m      \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 3. **Identify the End Behavior:**
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    From the analysis, as \( x \) approaches \( 3 \) from either side, the function approaches \( +\infty \) or \( -\infty \). This means that along the vertical lines \( x = 3 \), the function values can be arbitrarily large or small but will never touch or cross the x-axis (i.e., never equal to \( 0 \)).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 4. **Determine the Range:**
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    Since the function extends infinitely above and below the line \( y = 0 \), it can take all values greater than or equal to 0 without reaching 0. Therefore, the range of \( k(x) \) is:
