[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m The line with equation $y = x$ is an axis of symmetry of the curve with equation
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[y = \frac{px + q}{rx + s},\]where $p,$ $q,$ $r,$ $s$ are all nonzero.  Which of the following statements must hold?
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m (A) $p + q = 0$
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m (B) $p + r = 0$
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m (C) $p + s = 0$
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m (D) $q + r = 0$
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m (E) $q + s = 0$
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m (F) $r + s = 0$
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m At first glance, it looks like the simplest task is to write down that every other line passing through $(0,y)$ is the $y - x$ line (straight line passing through $({0},y)$ is just with zero coefficient terms, and as all lines passing through $(0,y)$ are $y - x$.)  Then, check if it satisfies the requirement of any point to satisfy the condition of the line of symmetry, by substituting $(x',y')$ into the formula.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Since the last term should not change when the $(x,y)$ and $(x',y')$ become a tilted point $(m,n)$, it naturally among all.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Since we need to find $p,r$ and $s$ those are parameters,therefore we could have expected accurate value, $$(x,y)=\left(\frac{pk - q}{(p-1)r},\frac{qs - py}{(p-1)r}\right).$$
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Now translate everything to terms of $p$,$q$,$r$,$s$,
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[y=x \Rightarrow \frac{pq}{s}=x.\]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Then from $(2)$ of the $y - x$ equation, we know that $x'=1$.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Substitute to $y=x$, we have $\frac{p}{p-1}\frac{q-1}{s}=s-1.$
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Then consider $(3)$ of $y = \frac{px + q}{rx + s}$, we have $$ y=\frac{p(s + 1)(x') + q}{(r + 1)s(p + r)},$$
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m so we have $$\frac{q-1}{p-1}\frac{s+1}{(r+s)(p+r)}=1.$$
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m So we have $\frac{q-1}{p-1} =\frac{s+1}{(r+s)(p+r)}.$
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m [distance=$d\overrightarrow{ab}$=|$cd-a b$|$ \sqrt{(c-a)^{2}+ 0-$, $\sqrt{(d-b)^{2}+0}$.=$\frac{d a-b c}{\overrightarrow{ab}\cdot\overrightarrow{ad}-\,\overrightarrow{ab}\cdot\overrightarrow{ac}}$ ](Ikk4) smallest AC median squared to ahos
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \begin{aligned} &\Rightarrow \frac{q-1}{r-1} =\frac{s+1}{(r+s)(p+r)}\\ & \Leftrightarrow \Delta s (2 rs +1)\frac{q-1}{r-1} =\left(1-\frac{qr}{r-1}\right)(1-\frac{1}{r-1})\\ &\Leftrightarrow \Delta (\frac{1}{r}\frac{q-1}{1-\frac{ q-r}{r+1}}) \geq 0\\ & \mid \because  q-1 \geq 0\\ & \Rightarrow\frac{q-1}{r-1}\geq 1,\text{ and}\frac{1}{r}\frac{q-1}{1-\frac{ q-r}{r+1}}\geq 1, \text {which completes the proof (D) }
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \end{aligned}<|endoftext|>
