[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m A population of bacteria is doubling every four hours. At noon there are $600$ bacteria. If this rate continues, how many bacteria will be present at midnight, twelve hours later?
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To determine the number of bacteria present at midnight, we need to use the exponential growth formula:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ P(t) = P_0 \cdot 2^{t/T} \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m where:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - \( P(t) \) is the number of bacteria at time \( t \),
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - \( P_0 \) is the initial number of bacteria,
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - \( t \) is the time in hours,
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - \( T \) is the time it takes for the population to double.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m In this problem:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - The initial number of bacteria \( P_0 \) is 600.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - The time间隔 \( T \) is 14 hours (since 14 hours is 12 + 2), because 12 hours is the doubling time of 2, and twice that is 24 hours, which is 14 hours.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Substituting these values into the formula, we get:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ P(14) = 600 \cdot 2^{14/14} \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ P(14) = 600 \cdot 2^1 \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ P(14) = 600 \cdot 2 \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ P(14) = 1200 \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Therefore, the number of bacteria present at midnight, twelve hours later, is \(\boxed{1200}\).<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
