[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To determine the type of curve on which the points of intersection of the lines \( tx - 2y - 3t = 0 \) and \( x - 2ty + 3 = 0 \) lie, we need to solve these linear equations simultaneously.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m First, we start with the equations:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m tx - 2y - 3t = 0 \quad \text{(1)}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m x - 2ty + 3 = 0 \quad \text{(2)}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m From equation (1), we can isolate \( t \):
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m tx = 2y + 3t \implies t(x - 2) = 3y \implies t = \frac{3y}{x - 2}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Next, we substitute \( t = \frac{3y}{x - 2} \) into equation (2):
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m x - 2 \left( \frac{3y}{x - 2} \right) y + 3 = 0
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m x - \frac{6y^2}{x - 2} + 3 = 0
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m We can combine the terms over a common denominator:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m x - \frac{6y^2}{x - 2} + 3 = 0
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Multiply through by \( x - 2 \) to clear the denominator:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m x(x - 2) - 6y^2 + 3(x - 2) = 0
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m x^2 - 2x - 6y^2 + 3x - 6 = 0
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m x^2 + x - 3y^2 - 6 = 0
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Rearrange the equation to standard form:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m x^2 - 3y^2 = 6
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m This is the equation of an ellipse. Therefore, the points of intersection lie on an ellipse.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m The correct option is \(\boxed{\text{E}}\).<|endoftext|>
