[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To determine how many of the four envelopes (A, B, C, and D) must pay the extra $0.11 in postage, we need to compare the ratio of the length to the height of each envelope with the given threshold of $0.11$.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m ### Step 1: Calculate the Ratio for Each Envelope
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m For each envelope:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \text{Length} = 6 \, \text{inches}, \quad \text{Height} = 4 \, \text{inches}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \text{Ratio} = \frac{\text{Length}}{\text{Height}} = \frac{6}{4} = 1.5
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m For each envelope except for the one we will check:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \text{Length} = 9 \, \text{inches}, \quad \text{Height} = 3 \, \text{inches}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \text{Ratio} = \frac{9}{3} = 3
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m For envelope D (with a length of 11 inches):
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \text{Length} = 11 \, \text{inches}, \quad \text{Height} = 4 \, \text{inches}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \text{Ratio} = \frac{11}{4} = 2.75
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m ### Step 2: Determine Which Envelopes Pass the Threshold
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - Envelope A with length 6 inches and height 4 inches: Ratio = 1.5 (pass threshold)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - Envelope B with length 9 inches and height 3 inches: Ratio = 3 (pass threshold)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - Envelope C with length 6 inches and height 6 inches: Ratio = 3 (pass threshold)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - Envelope D with length 11 inches and height 4 inches: Ratio = 2.75 (light)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - Envelope E with length 9 inches: Ratio = 3 (pass threshold)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - Envelope F with length 11 inches: Ratio = 2.66 (light)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - Envelope G with length 6 inches and height 6 inches: Ratio = 3 (pass threshold)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m From the above calculations, we find that the envelopes with lengths 6 inches and 11 inches pass the threshold, and those with lengths 9 inches and 11 inches light the threshold.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Statistically, for envelopes with lengths less than 8 inches or more than 5 inches, they are considered light.
