[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To find the perimeter of a triangle, we need to add up the lengths of all its sides. The triangle given has sides of length 14 cm, 8 cm, and 9 cm.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Step 1: Identify all the side lengths.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - First side = 14 cm
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - Second side = 8 cm
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - Third side = 9 cm
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Step 2: Add the lengths of all three sides.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ 14 \, \text{cm} + 8 \, \text{cm} + 9 \, \text{cm} \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Step 3: Perform the addition.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ 14 + 8 = 22 \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ 22 + 9 = 31 \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m So, the perimeters of the triangle are:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ 31 \, \text{cm} \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Step 4: Put the final answer in the required format.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \boxed{31}<|endoftext|>
