[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Tamika selects two different numbers at random from the set $\{8,9,10\}$ and adds them. Carlos takes two different numbers at random from the set $\{3,5,6\}$ and multiplies them. What is the probability that Tamika's result is greater than Carlos' result?  Express your answer as a common fraction.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To determine the probability that Tamika's result is greater than Carlos' result, we need to analyze the different possible outcomes and their corresponding sums and products.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m First, let's list all possible pairs \((a, b)\) where \(a\) is a number chosen from the set \(\{8, 9, 10\}\) and \(b\) is a number chosen from the set \(\{3, 5, 6\}\):
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m (8, 3), (8, 5), (8, 6), (9, 3), (9, 5), (9, 6), (10, 3), (10, 5), (10, 6)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Next, we calculate the sum and product for each pair:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \begin{aligned}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m &\text{Sum} = 8 + 3 = 11, \quad \text{Product} = 8 \times 3 = 24, \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m &\text{Sum} = 8 + 5 = 13, \quad \text{Product} = 8 \times 5 = 40, \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m &\text{Sum} = 8 + 6 = 14, \quad \text{Product} = 8 \times 6 = 48, \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m &\text{Sum} = 9 + 3 = 12, \quad \text{Product} = 9 \times 3 = 27, \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m &\text{Sum} = 9 + 5 = 14, \quad \text{Product} = 9 \times 5 = 45, \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m &\text{Sum} = 9 + 6 = 15, \quad \text{Product} = 9 \times 6 = 54, \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m &\text{Sum} = 10 + 3 = 13, \quad \text{Product} = 10 \times 3 = 30, \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m &\text{Sum} = 10 + 5 = 15, \quad \text{Product} = 10 \times 5 = 50, \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m &\text{Sum} = 10 + 6 = 16, \quad \text{Product} = 10 \times 6 = 60.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \end{aligned}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Now, we list the sums of the pairs along with their products:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \begin{aligned}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m &11 & 24 & 40 & 13 & 40 & 48 & 27 & 45 & 54 & 30 & 15 & 50 & 16 & 60 \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m &91 & 194 & 81 & 14 & 84 & 126 & 27 & 36 & 225 & 75 & 15 & 55 & 18 & 30 \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m &9 & 24 & 40 & 13 & 40 & 48 & 27 & 45 & 54 & 30 & 15 & 50 & 16 & 60
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \end{aligned}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m We see that the only pair where Tamika's result is greater than Carlos' result is \((9, 10)\). There are 8 such pairs out of a total of 19 possible pairs.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m The probability is then the number of favorable outcomes divided by the total number of outcomes:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \frac{8}{19}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Thus, the probability that Tamika's result is greater than Carlos' result is:
