[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To evaluate \(\lfloor 3.2 \rfloor\), we need to determine the greatest integer that is less than or equal to 3.2. The floor function, denoted by \(\lfloor x \rfloor\), returns the greatest integer that is no greater than \(x\).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Here are the steps to find \(\lfloor 3.2 \rfloor\):
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 1. Identify the integer part of 3.2. The integer part of a number \(x\) is \(k\), where \(k\) is the greatest integer such that \(k \leq x\).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 2. Compare 3.2 to the integer values that make the inequality \(k \leq x\). For 3.2, it is less than all the integer values (3, 2, 1, 0, -1, -2, -3, -4, -5, -6, -7, ...) because the smallest integer that is still greater than or equal to 3.2 is 3.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 3. Hence, \(\lfloor 3.2 \rfloor = 3\).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
