[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To find the determinant of the matrix \(\mathbf{R}\) which rotates counter-clockwise by an angle of \(58^\circ\), we can use the formula for the determinant of a rotation matrix. For a \(2 \times 2\) rotation matrix \(\mathbf{R}\) given by:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \mathbf{R} = \begin{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \cos \theta & -\sin \theta \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \sin \theta & \cos \theta
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \end{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m where \(\theta\) is the angle of rotation, and \(\cos \theta\) and \(\sin \theta\) are the components of the rotation vector, we have:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \mathbf{R} = \begin{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \cos \theta & -\sin \theta \\
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \sin \theta & \cos \theta
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \end{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m The determinant of matrix \(\mathbf{R}\) is given by:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \det \mathbf{R} = \cos^2 \theta + \sin^2 \theta
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m From the definition of the cosine and sine functions, we know that:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \cos^2 \theta + \sin^2 \theta = 1
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Thus, the determinant of the rotation matrix \(\mathbf{R}\) is:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
