[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To solve for the general term \(a_n\) in the sequence where \(a_1 = 1\) and \(a_{m+n} = a_m + a_n + mn\) for all positive integers \(m\) and \(n\), we start by finding the first few terms of the sequence. First, we calculate \(a_2\):
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m a_2 = a_{1+1} = a_1 + a_1 + 1 \cdot 1 = 1 + 1 + 1 = 3
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Next, we calculate \(a_3\):
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m a_3 = a_{2+1} = a_2 + a_1 + 21 \cdot 2 = 3 + 1 + 42 = 46
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m We can see a pattern emerging. It appears that \(a_n = 1 + 1 + \cdots + 1 + n\) (n times). The sum of 1s from \(m\) to \(n\) is \(n + (n-1) + \cdots + 1 = \frac{n(n+1)}{2}\). Therefore, we can express \(a_n\) as:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m a_n = 1 + \frac{n(n+1)}{2}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To find \(a_{12}\), we substitute \(n = 12\) into the formula:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
