[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m For the opening home game of the baseball season, the Madd Batters minor league baseball team offered the following incentives to its fans:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Every 75th fan who entered the stadium got a coupon for a free hot dog.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Every 30th fan who entered the stadium got a coupon for a free cup of soda.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Every 50th fan who entered the stadium got a coupon for a free bag of popcorn.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m The stadium holds 4000 fans and was completely full for this game. How many of the fans at the game were lucky enough to receive all three free items?
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Let's denote the number of fans who received all three Items as \( x \). These fans will be lucky enough to get all three items:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 1. $75$ items which are $75x$.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 2. $30$ items which are $30x$.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 3. $50$ items which are $50x$.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Since the total number of items a fan gets is the sum of these three quantities, we have:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 155x = 4000
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Solving for \( x \), we multiply both sides by \(\frac{1}{155}\):
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
