[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To find the range of the function \( f(x) = \begin{cases} x^2 & \text{if } x \le 0 \\ x^3 - 5 & \text{if } x > 0 \end{cases} \), we need to determine the possible values that \( f(x) \) can take.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m ### Step 1: Analyze the case \( x \le 0 \)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m For \( x \le 0 \), the function is given by \( f(x) = x^2 \). Since \( x^2 \) is a square of a real number, it is always non-negative and can take any non-negative value, including 0. Therefore, for \( x \le 0 \), the possible values of \( f(x) \) are:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ f(x) = 0 \text{ for } x \le 0. \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m ### Step 2: Analyze the case \( x > 0 \)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m For \( x > 0 \), the function is given by \( f(x) = x^3 - 5 \). To find the range of this function for \( x > 0 \), we need to determine the range of the quadratic function \( g(x) = x^3 - 5 \).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m The function \( g(x) = x^3 - 5 \) is a cubic function with a global minimum at \( x = 0 \) because the derivative \( g'(x) = 3x^2 \) is zero at \( x = 0 \) and positive for \( x > 0 \). Therefore, the quadratic function \( g(x) = x^3 - 5 \) attains its minimum value at \( x = 0 \) and is increasing for \( x > 0 \). For \( x > 0 \), the maximum value of \( g(x) \) is just below 5, and it can take any value less than or equal to 5. Therefore, for \( x > 0 \), the possible values of \( f(x) \) are:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ g(x) \text{ for } x > 0 \text{ can take any value } y \text{ where } 0 \leq y \leq 5. \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m ### Step 3: Combine the results
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Combining the results from both cases, we see that the range of the function \( f(x) \) is all real numbers in the interval \( [0, 5] \). Therefore, the range of \( f(x) \) is:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ \boxed{[0, 5]} \]<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
