[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To compute \(\tan 330^\circ\), we will follow a step-by-step approach.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m ### Step 1: Understanding the Angle
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m The angle \(330^\circ\) is in the third quadrant of the unit circle. In the unit circle, angles are measured from the positive x-axis in the counterclockwise direction. The reference angle for \(330^\circ\) is as follows:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 360^\circ - 330^\circ = 30^\circ
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Since the angle is in the third quadrant, we need to add \(180^\circ\):
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 330^\circ + 180^\circ = 510^\circ
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Thus, \(330^\circ\) is equivalent to \(510^\circ\) in the third quadrant.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m ### Step 2: Using the Tangent Value
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m The tangent of an angle is given by:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \tan \theta = \frac{\sin \theta}{\cos \theta}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m For the reference angle of \(30^\circ\), we know:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \tan 30^\circ = \frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m ### Step 3: Simplifying the Tangent of \(330^\circ\)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To find \(\tan 330^\circ\), we need to reduce the angle to the equivalent angle in the interval \([0^\circ, 180^\circ]\). Since \(330^\circ\) is in the third quadrant, we add \(180^\circ\):
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 330^\circ + 180^\circ = 510^\circ
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Thus, \(\tan 330^\circ = \tan 510^\circ\).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m ### Step 4: Expressing \(\tan 510^\circ\) in Terms of \(\tan 90^\circ\)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m The tangent function has special values at \(90^\circ\) and \(270^\circ\):
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \tan 90^\circ \text{ is undefined}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \tan 270^\circ = \text{undefined}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Since \(\tan 330^\circ = \tan (90^\circ - 540^\circ)\), we can use the tangent subtraction formula:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \tan (90^\circ - \theta) = \frac{\tan 90^\circ}{1 - \tan^2 \theta}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Here, \(\theta = 540^\circ\), and \(\tan 90^\circ\) is undefined, so we simplify:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \tan (90^\circ - 540^\circ) = \frac{\tan 0^\circ}{1 - \tan^2 0^\circ} = \frac{0}{0 - 1^2} = 0
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m ### Step 5: Conclusion
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Thus, \(\tan 330^\circ = 0\).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
