[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m A world-record swimmer and a dolphin compete in a 400-meter race. The swimmer swims at a rate of 2 meters per second, and the dolphin swims at a rate of 8 meters per second. When the dolphin crosses the finish line, how many meters is the swimmer from the finish line?
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To determine how many meters the swimmer is from the finish line, we need to use the concept of relative speed. The swimmer is moving at 2 meters per second, and the dolphin is moving at 8 meters per second. Since the dolphin is faster, the swimmer will be overtaken while the dolphin is in the process of crossing the finish line.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m First, calculate the relative speed of the swimmer with respect to the dolphin:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ 8 \text{ meters/second} - 2 \text{ meters/second} = 6 \text{ meters/second} \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m This means that every second, the swimmer closes the gap of 6 meters relative to the dolphin.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To find out how much the swimmer will have moved when the dolphin finishes, we need to determine how much the relative speed affects the 400-meter lap. Since the swimmer is half-way through the 400-meter lap (because it's a 400-meter race), we can think of the race as moving from half-way to the very end of the 400-meter lap.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Given that the relative speed is 6 meters per second, and the distance to be covered is 400 meters, we can calculate the time it takes for the relative speed to cover the 400-meter distance:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ \text{Time} = \frac{\text{Distance}}{\text{Relative Speed}} = \frac{400 \text{ meters}}{6 \text{ meters/second}} = \frac{400}{6} = \frac{200}{3} \text{ seconds} \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Now, we need to find out how far the swimmer will have swum in this time. Since the relative speed is 6 meters per second, and it takes \(\frac{200}{3}\) seconds to cover the 400 meters, the swimmer will have swum:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ \text{Distance} = \text{Relative Speed} \times \text{Time} = 6 \text{ meters/second} \times \frac{200}{3} \text{ seconds} = \frac{1200}{3} = 400 \text{ meters} \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Therefore, the swimmer is \(\boxed{400}\) meters from the finish line when the dolphin finishes crossing the finish line.<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m [Ground Truth]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 300
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m [Is Boxed Matched]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m True
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m [Extracted Answer]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 400
