[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m For the opening home game of the baseball season, the Madd Batters minor league baseball team offered the following incentives to its fans:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Every 75th fan who entered the stadium got a coupon for a free hot dog.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Every 30th fan who entered the stadium got a coupon for a free cup of soda.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Every 50th fan who entered the stadium got a coupon for a free bag of popcorn.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m The stadium holds 4000 fans and was completely full for this game. How many of the fans at the game were lucky enough to receive all three free items?
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To determine the number of fans who received all three items, we need to find the least common multiple (LCM) of the three numbers 75, 30, and 50. The LCM of these numbers will give us the number of fans who receive all three items.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m First, let's find the prime factorization of each number:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - \(75 = 3 \times 5^2\)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - \(30 = 2 \times 3 \times 5\)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - \(50 = 2 \times 5^2\)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m The LCM is found by taking the highest power of each prime that appears in the factorizations:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - The highest power of 2 is \(2^1\)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - The highest power of 3 is \(3^1\)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - The highest power of 5 is \(5^2\)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m So, the LCM is:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ \text{LCM} = 2^1 \times 3^1 \times 5^2 = 2 \times 3 \times 25 = 150 \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Therefore, every 150th fan (between the 4000th and 4001st) receives all three items, and no fan receives all three items if they are not within the range of 4000 to 4001. To find how many fans were lucky enough to receive all three items, we divide 4000 by 150:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ \left\lfloor \frac{4000}{150} \right\rfloor = 26 \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m So, the number of fans who were lucky enough to receive all three items is \(\boxed{26}\).<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m [Ground Truth]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 26
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m [Is Boxed Matched]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m True
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
