[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To find \(\sec 120^\circ\), we start by recalling the definition of secant. The secant of an angle \(\theta\) is the reciprocal of the cosine of that angle, i.e., \(\sec \theta = \frac{1}{\cos \theta}\).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m First, we need to determine \(\cos 120^\circ\). The angle \(120^\circ\) is in the second quadrant, where the cosine is negative. The reference angle for \(120^\circ\) is \(180^\circ - 120^\circ = 60^\circ\), but since \(120^\circ\) is in the second quadrant, the cosine of \(120^\circ\) is the same as the cosine of its reference angle, which is \(-\frac{1}{2}\).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Now, we can find \(\sec 120^\circ\):
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \sec 120^\circ = \frac{1}{\cos 120^\circ} = \frac{1}{-\frac{1}{2}} = -2
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Therefore, the value of \(\sec 120^\circ\) is \(\boxed{-2}\).<|endoftext|>
