[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To simplify \(\sin 17^\circ + \sin 43^\circ\), we can use the sum-to-product identities for sine. The identity for the sum of sines is:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \sin A + \sin B = 2 \sin \left( \frac{A + B}{2} \right) \cos \left( \frac{A - B}{2} \right)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Here, \(A = 17^\circ\) and \(B = 43^\circ\). Plugging in these values, we get:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \sin 17^\circ + \sin 43^\circ = 2 \sin \left( \frac{17^\circ + 43^\circ}{2} \right) \cos \left( \frac{17^\circ - 43^\circ}{2} \right)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Simplify the arguments of the sine and cosine functions:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \frac{17^\circ + 43^\circ}{2} = \frac{60^\circ}{2} = 30^\circ
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \frac{17^\circ - 43^\circ}{2} = \frac{-26^\circ}{2} = -13^\circ
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m So the expression becomes:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \sin 17^\circ + \sin 43^\circ = 2 \sin 30^\circ \cos (-13^\circ)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m We know that \(\sin 30^\circ = \frac{1}{2}\). Therefore:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 2 \sin 30^\circ \cos (-13^\circ) = 2 \left( \frac{1}{2} \right) \cos (-13^\circ) = \cos (-13^\circ)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Since \(\cos (-13^\circ) = \cos 13^\circ\), we have:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \cos (-13^\circ) = \cos 13^\circ
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Thus, the simplified form of \(\sin 17^\circ + \sin 43^\circ\) is:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \boxed{\cos 13^\circ}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
