[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To find the domain of the function \( g(x) = \frac{x^3 + 11x - 2}{|x - 3| + |x + 1|} \), we need to ensure that the denominator is not zero. The domain of a rational function is all real numbers except where the denominator is zero.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Let's start by analyzing the denominator:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ |x - 3| + |x + 1|. \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m The expression inside the absolute value bars is always non-negative, so the denominator will be zero when:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ |x - 3| + |x + 1| = 0. \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m This equation holds if both \( |x - 3| = 0 \) and \( |x + 1| = 0 \). Therefore, we have:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ x = 3 \quad \text{and} \quad x = -1. \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m So, the only value that satisfies the equation is \( x = 3 \) or \( x = -1 \). Therefore, the denominator is zero at these points, and the domain of the function will be all real numbers except \( x = 3 \) and \( x = -1 \).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m In interval notation, the domain is:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[ (-\infty, -1) \cup (-1, 3) \cup (3, \infty). \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Thus, the domain of the function is:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \boxed{(-\infty, -1) \cup (-1, 3) \cup (3, \infty)}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m [Ground Truth]
