[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To find \( f(f(f(2))) \) for the function \( f(x) = 5x - 4 \), we need to evaluate the function step by step from the innermost function to the outermost function. Let's start from the innermost function and work our way out.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 1. Evaluate \( f(2) \):
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    f(2) = 5(2) - 4 = 10 - 4 = 6
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 2. Next, we use the result from the first step to evaluate \( f(f(2)) \), which is \( f(6) \):
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    f(6) = 5(6) - 4 = 30 - 4 = 26
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 3. Finally, we use the result from the second step to evaluate \( f(f(f(2))) \), which is \( f(26) \):
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    f(26) = 5(26) - 4 = 130 - 4 = 126
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m So, the value of \( f(f(f(2))) \) is \(\boxed{126}\).<|endoftext|>
