[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To find the 111th digit after the decimal point when \(\frac{33}{555}\) is expressed as a decimal, we first need to find the decimal representation of \(\frac{33}{555}\).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m We start by performing the division \(33 \div 555\):
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 1. \(33 \div 555 \approx 0.06\)
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m So, \(\frac{33}{555} = 0.06\).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m This means that \(\frac{33}{555}\) is the decimal 0.06. Since the decimal repeats every two digits, the repeating sequence is "060606...".
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To find the 111th digit after the decimal point, we need to determine the position of the 111th digit within the repeating sequence "060606...". We do this by finding the remainder when 111 is divided by 2:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[111 \div 2 = 55 \text{ remainder } 1.\]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m This tells us that 111 is 1 more than a multiple of 2. Therefore, the 111th digit is the same as the 1st digit of the repeating sequence "06", which is 0.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Thus, the 111th digit after the decimal point is \(\boxed{0}\).<|endoftext|>
