[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To solve the problem, we need to find the point \( P = (p, 0) \) such that for any chord \(\overline{AB}\) passing through \( F = (1,0) \), the angles \(\angle APF\) and \(\angle BPF\) are equal. This means that the ratios of the segments on either side of point \( F \) should be proportional.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Given the ellipse equation \(\frac{x^2}{2} + y^2 = 1\), we know that the foci of the ellipse are located at \((\pm 1, 0)\). For any chord \(\overline{AB}\) passing through \( F = (1,0) \), the tangent to the ellipse at point \( P = (p, 0) \) will be vertical if we consider the other two possible tangents formed by the chord.
