[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To determine the area of the largest possible inscribed triangle in a circle, we start by noting that the largest such inscribed triangle is a right triangle with the hypotenuse as the diameter of the circle. Given that the radius of the circle is 6 cm, the diameter of the circle is \(2 \times 6 = 12\) cm. Therefore, the hypotenuse of the largest inscribed triangle is 12 cm.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m We need to find the triangle that has one side as the diameter of the circle. In this case, the triangle will be a right triangle with the hypotenuse of 12 cm. The side lengths of the triangle will be equal to the radius of the circle, i.e., 6 cm, 6 cm, and 6 cm. The area \(A\) of a right triangle is given by the formula:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m A = \frac{1}{2} \times \text{base} \times \text{height}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Here, the base and height are both equal to the radius of the circle, which is 6 cm. Substituting these values into the formula, we get:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m A = \frac{1}{2} \times 6 \times 6 = \frac{1}{2} \times 36 = 18 \text{ cm}^2
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Since the circle is symmetric and the triangle is inscribed in it, the area of the largest possible inscribed triangle is simply the area of this right triangle. Therefore, the area of the largest possible inscribed triangle is:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \boxed{18}
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
