[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m To find the value of \( x \), we need to use the fact that the sum of the angles in a triangle is always \( 180^\circ \).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Given:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - The triangle is isosceles with angles \( 50^\circ \) at the base and \( 60^\circ \) at the vertex.
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m - The base angles are each \( 50^\circ \).
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Let the vertex angle be \( x \). According to the angle sum property of a triangle:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 50^\circ + 50^\circ + x = 180^\circ
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m Simplify the equation:
[36m(main_task pid=251513)[0m \[
